{
  "image_name": "8573059256e637428dac1b1a74fab4e4.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how silk hydrogels are formed and tuned through several crosslinking strategies. At the top, a silk solution in a mold is converted into a hydrogel by adding gelation agents. The main comparison below shows four network-forming mechanisms within tangled silk chains: physical crosslinking, dityrosine crosslinking, click chemistry, and supramolecular assembly.\n\nPhysical crosslinking appears to rely on external triggers such as temperature, pH, sonication, or vortexing, which drive silk chains from random coils into β-sheet crystal domains that act as junctions. Dityrosine crosslinking instead forms covalent links between tyrosine residues, with schemes suggesting peroxide/HRP or other oxidative methods. Click chemistry introduces reactive motifs onto silk and links them with multifunctional partners, creating a more designed covalent network. Supramolecular gelation uses reversible host–guest interactions, illustrated with macrocycles such as β-cyclodextrin binding small guest molecules.\n\nThe overall message is that silk hydrogels can be engineered through either physical, covalent, or reversible interactions, and that each route depends on different design parameters. A final box emphasizes the resulting biomedical properties that can be controlled, including mechanics, pore size, rheology, self-healing, biodegradability, swelling, and bioactivity.",
